<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-nonhomogeneous-ivp" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Solving an IVP with a Non-Homogeneous Equation</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: REMIND OF GENERAL SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    On the previous page we found the general solution to
    <me>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t),</me>
    which is
    <me>y = \underbrace{c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t}}_{y_c} + \underbrace{\left(-\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t)\right)}_{y_p}.</me>
    Now we go one step further: given initial conditions, we can solve for <m>c_1</m> and
    <m>c_2</m> to find the unique solution to an IVP.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: IVP
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="example-nonhomogeneous-ivp">
    <title>Solving an IVP for a Non-Homogeneous Equation</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find the specific solution to
        <me>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t)</me>
        satisfying <m>y(0) = 0</m> and <m>y'(0) = 2</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── Part (a): MC checkpoint on where ICs go ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp-where-ics-go">
      <title>Where do the initial conditions go?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          We know the general solution is <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>, where <m>y_c = c_1 e^t + c_2 e^{2t}</m>
          and <m>y_p = -\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t)</m>.
          To find <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, which expression should we plug the initial conditions into?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>The full general solution <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! The initial conditions must be satisfied by the actual solution <m>y</m>,
              which is the full <m>y_c + y_p</m>. Plugging into just <m>y_c</m> would ignore
              the contribution of <m>y_p</m> at <m>t = 0</m> and give the wrong values of
              <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Just <m>y_c = c_1 e^t + c_2 e^{2t}</m>, since that's where <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> live.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This is a very common mistake! It's true that <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> appear
              only in <m>y_c</m>, but the initial conditions must be satisfied by the full solution
              <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>. Ignoring <m>y_p</m> when applying the ICs will give
              incorrect values of <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Just <m>y_p</m>, since it's the solution to the non-homogeneous equation.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>y_p</m> is a particular solution but it has no free constants, so we can't
              adjust it to satisfy arbitrary initial conditions. We need the full <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Plug the ICs into <m>y_c</m> first to find <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, then add <m>y_p</m>.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This gets the order wrong. We first write out the full <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>,
              then plug the ICs into that. Solving <m>y_c</m> alone for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>
              and adding <m>y_p</m> afterward will not produce a solution that satisfies the ICs.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>



    <!-- ── Part (b): Find c1 and c2, solve IVP ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-ivp-solve">
      <title>Finding <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the initial conditions <m>y(0) = 0</m> and <m>y'(0) = 2</m> to find
          <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, and state the specific solution to the IVP.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Answer:</alert>
          <me>y = -\frac{4}{5}e^{t} + \frac{3}{2}e^{2t} - \frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t).</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Start with the general solution and its derivative:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>y = c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t} - \dfrac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \dfrac{1}{10}\sin(2t)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y'(t) = c_1 e^{t} + 2c_2 e^{2t} + \dfrac{7}{5}\sin(2t) - \dfrac{1}{5}\cos(2t)</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Then plug in <m>t = 0</m> using each initial condition to get a system of two equations in <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p>From <m>y(0) = 0</m>: plug <m>t=0</m> into <m>y</m> and set equal to <m>0</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>From <m>y'(0) = 2</m>: plug <m>t=0</m> into <m>y'</m> and set equal to <m>2</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
          Solve the resulting system for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <title>Full solution</title>
        <p>
          We start with the general solution and its derivative:
          <md>
            <mrow>y \amp= c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t} - \frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t)</mrow>
            <mrow>y'(t) \amp= c_1 e^{t} + 2c_2 e^{2t} + \frac{7}{5}\sin(2t) - \frac{1}{5}\cos(2t)</mrow>
          </md>
          Plugging in <m>t = 0</m> and applying each initial condition:
          <md>
            <mrow>y(0) = 0 \amp\implies c_1 + c_2 - \frac{7}{10} = 0 \implies c_1 + c_2 = \frac{7}{10}</mrow>
            <mrow>y'(0) = 2 \amp\implies c_1 + 2c_2 - \frac{1}{5} = 2 \implies c_1 + 2c_2 = \frac{11}{5}</mrow>
          </md>
          So we need to solve the system:
          <md>
            <mrow>c_1 + c_2 \amp= \frac{7}{10}</mrow>
            <mrow>c_1 + 2c_2 \amp= \frac{11}{5}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Solving the system for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> (can skim)</title>
          <p>
            We subtract the first equation from the second to eliminate <m>c_1</m>:
            <md>
              <mrow>(c_1 + 2c_2) - (c_1 + c_2) \amp= \frac{11}{5} - \frac{7}{10}</mrow>
              <mrow>c_2 \amp= \frac{22}{10} - \frac{7}{10} = \frac{15}{10} = \frac{3}{2}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Substituting <m>c_2 = \frac{3}{2}</m> back into the first equation:
            <md>
              <mrow>c_1 + \frac{3}{2} \amp= \frac{7}{10}</mrow>
              <mrow>c_1 \amp= \frac{7}{10} - \frac{15}{10} = -\frac{4}{5}.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          This gives <m>c_1 = -\dfrac{4}{5}</m> and <m>c_2 = \dfrac{3}{2}</m>, so the specific solution to the IVP is:
          <me>y = \underbrace{-\frac{4}{5}}_{c_1}e^{t} + \underbrace{\frac{3}{2}}_{c_2}e^{2t} + \underbrace{\left(-\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t)\right)}_{y_p}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The full process:</alert> To solve an IVP for a non-homogeneous equation,
          first find the general solution <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>, then plug the initial
          conditions into the <em>full</em> <m>y = y_c + y_p</m> (not just <m>y_c</m>)
          to solve for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Common mistake:</alert> Applying initial conditions to <m>y_c</m> alone
          ignores the constant value of <m>y_p</m> at <m>t = 0</m> and gives the wrong
          constants.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <remark>
    <p>
      Note: there is a small typo in the video for this page; the final answer should have
      <m>c_1 = -\frac{4}{5}</m>, not <m>c_1 = \frac{4}{5}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

